WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:03.050
A delivery company plans routes for

2
00:00:03.050 --> 00:00:05.570
two thousand vans every night.

3
00:00:06.200 --> 00:00:08.300
Nobody proves those routes are the

4
00:00:08.300 --> 00:00:10.440
best, and nobody needs to.

5
00:00:11.020 --> 00:00:13.800
They’re good, fast and checked.

6
00:00:14.510 --> 00:00:17.170
Today: how, and what a modeller

7
00:00:17.170 --> 00:00:18.510
owes the people affected.

8
00:00:19.820 --> 00:00:22.900
Today: heuristics, local search, and

9
00:00:22.900 --> 00:00:24.420
the trade-offs a modeller makes.

10
00:00:25.690 --> 00:00:29.030
Routing is NP-hard: as the stops

11
00:00:29.030 --> 00:00:31.750
grow, every known exact method can

12
00:00:31.750 --> 00:00:33.610
take exponentially long.

13
00:00:34.240 --> 00:00:36.680
Twenty villages already give more than

14
00:00:36.680 --> 00:00:38.780
a billion billion round trips.

15
00:00:39.400 --> 00:00:42.180
So for big instances, we trade a

16
00:00:42.180 --> 00:00:44.500
proof for a good answer in time.

17
00:00:45.770 --> 00:00:47.790
Heuristics work in two stages.

18
00:00:48.650 --> 00:00:51.770
First build a plan fast, for example

19
00:00:51.770 --> 00:00:54.570
nearest neighbour: always drive to the

20
00:00:54.570 --> 00:00:56.450
closest stop not yet visited.

21
00:00:57.090 --> 00:00:58.910
Then improve it with local

22
00:00:58.910 --> 00:01:01.170
search: change it a little, and

23
00:01:01.170 --> 00:01:02.610
keep the changes that help.

24
00:01:03.870 --> 00:01:06.690
For routes, the classic small change is

25
00:01:06.690 --> 00:01:10.470
2-opt: remove two roads, and reconnect by

26
00:01:10.470 --> 00:01:12.030
reversing the stretch between them.

27
00:01:12.560 --> 00:01:14.180
It removes every crossing.

28
00:01:14.990 --> 00:01:16.810
In the lab’s bus map, it takes

29
00:01:16.810 --> 00:01:18.330
nearest neighbour’s thirty-four

30
00:01:18.330 --> 00:01:20.190
point six kilometres down to

31
00:01:20.190 --> 00:01:22.770
twenty-eight point six, the best route.

32
00:01:23.980 --> 00:01:26.020
The heart of 2-opt is one

33
00:01:26.020 --> 00:01:27.940
line: reverse a slice.

34
00:01:28.740 --> 00:01:30.620
Keep the new route if the trip is

35
00:01:30.620 --> 00:01:33.820
shorter, and repeat until no swap helps.

36
00:01:35.090 --> 00:01:37.330
Local search stops at a local

37
00:01:37.330 --> 00:01:40.150
optimum, where no small change helps.

38
00:01:40.850 --> 00:01:43.990
Metaheuristics escape: annealing accepts

39
00:01:43.990 --> 00:01:46.770
some worse moves while hot, tabu search

40
00:01:46.770 --> 00:01:49.710
forbids undoing recent moves, and genetic

41
00:01:49.710 --> 00:01:51.550
algorithms mix good plans.

42
00:01:52.280 --> 00:01:54.560
In the seating lab, annealing finds

43
00:01:54.560 --> 00:01:56.860
the best plan two runs in three;

44
00:01:57.500 --> 00:01:59.340
hill climbing, one in four.

45
00:02:00.630 --> 00:02:01.210
Quick check!

46
00:02:02.000 --> 00:02:05.120
Your heuristic found a route of one

47
00:02:05.120 --> 00:02:07.940
hundred and five kilometres, and an

48
00:02:07.940 --> 00:02:10.400
LP bound says no route can be

49
00:02:10.400 --> 00:02:11.760
shorter than one hundred.

50
00:02:12.410 --> 00:02:14.710
What’s the optimality gap?

51
00:02:16.040 --> 00:02:18.920
About five percent: at most five

52
00:02:18.920 --> 00:02:21.400
kilometres could still be saved, and maybe

53
00:02:21.400 --> 00:02:21.740
none.

54
00:02:22.390 --> 00:02:24.790
A bound turns good into a

55
00:02:24.790 --> 00:02:25.730
measured promise.

56
00:02:27.140 --> 00:02:29.620
Real plans weigh several goals:

57
00:02:29.620 --> 00:02:32.580
total distance, the longest ride

58
00:02:32.580 --> 00:02:35.000
for any child, drivers’ hours.

59
00:02:35.770 --> 00:02:38.990
The shortest total can leave one village’s

60
00:02:38.990 --> 00:02:41.490
children on the bus for ninety minutes.

61
00:02:42.290 --> 00:02:44.810
Put the goals and the fairness limits in

62
00:02:44.810 --> 00:02:46.830
the model, and show the trade-off.

63
00:02:48.070 --> 00:02:50.610
In practice, OR-Tools’ routing library

64
00:02:50.610 --> 00:02:53.590
builds a first route, then improves it

65
00:02:53.590 --> 00:02:55.690
with guided local search within a time

66
00:02:55.690 --> 00:02:55.870
limit.

67
00:02:56.530 --> 00:02:59.050
Engineers test on past days, and

68
00:02:59.050 --> 00:03:00.930
keep a person able to override.

69
00:03:01.730 --> 00:03:03.350
Plans nobody can question

70
00:03:03.350 --> 00:03:04.610
don’t get trusted.

71
00:03:05.830 --> 00:03:08.670
In the app, seat twelve pupils with

72
00:03:08.670 --> 00:03:10.790
hill climbing and with annealing.

73
00:03:11.380 --> 00:03:14.040
Run each several times, and change

74
00:03:14.040 --> 00:03:15.140
the starting temperature.

75
00:03:16.310 --> 00:03:16.830
Your turn.

76
00:03:17.460 --> 00:03:20.900
Plan A is two kilometres shorter in total.

77
00:03:21.520 --> 00:03:24.680
Plan B is longer, but no child

78
00:03:24.680 --> 00:03:26.380
rides more than forty minutes.

79
00:03:27.160 --> 00:03:28.700
Which should the model pick?

80
00:03:30.000 --> 00:03:33.020
It depends on goals people agree, so

81
00:03:33.020 --> 00:03:34.600
show both, with their numbers.

82
00:03:35.340 --> 00:03:37.220
Remember: for hard problems,

83
00:03:37.640 --> 00:03:39.160
build, then improve.

84
00:03:40.030 --> 00:03:43.130
Escape local optima with metaheuristics,

85
00:03:43.410 --> 00:03:45.390
and measure the gap against a bound.

86
00:03:46.000 --> 00:03:47.380
And put fairness in the

87
00:03:47.380 --> 00:03:48.720
model, in the open.

88
00:03:50.030 --> 00:03:52.690
Try it yourself: write 2-opt for a

89
00:03:52.690 --> 00:03:54.890
five-stop route in Python, and check

90
00:03:54.890 --> 00:03:56.690
it against every possible route.

91
00:03:57.330 --> 00:03:59.250
Then add a limit on the longest

92
00:03:59.250 --> 00:04:01.130
ride, and see what it costs.
